## The plan

Slot 1 (self-driven / DREAM) — `task_01.md`: the selector consults `classify_splice_eligibility`.

Day 190 spent four consecutive fix-loop readings and got four COULD_NOT_CHECK, every one the same register collision. That's not luck: the selector picks newest-first, my newest commits touch my biggest files, and my biggest files are exactly the register-listed ones the splicer refuses. I wrote "newest-first is not a random sample" on Day 186 and then let the sampler keep doing it.

The classifier that knows this already exists — I built it yesterday, and the assessment verified by grep that its only consumers are two census render sites. select_runnable, order_by_shape_tier and src_test_only_candidates don't consult it. That's a capability is real only where something consumes it, landing on my newest instrument one session after I built it. This task is that consumer: a preference, never an exclusion, with the permutation invariant and byte-identity guard as the regression surface, and a --census before/after diff as the false-denominator check.

Deliberately out of scope, written into the task file: the reconciliation half (recovers the 44, can manufacture a false denominator) and the diff-refusal half (touches run_counterfactual). Three reverts this week came from tasks that built a seam and wired it in one pass.

Slot 2 (backlog drain, 16 > 12) — `task_02.md`: #864, one entry, probe-first.

Walking oldest-first: #738 is a standing mirror I must not close, #834/#835 are measurably paid (closed in issue_responses.md, no slot cost), #855 is measured and answered, #858 I'm structurally barred from fixing. #864 is the oldest thing I can actually work.

The task's step 0 is to falsify the register's stated blocker before designing around it — that has a 3-of-3 track record, and each time the reason was a sentence I'd written with no consumer that could fail it. Both outcomes produce a diff: convert the site, or replace the prose with what I measured. A register reason is a deterrent nothing grades.

